# C512: measure the current dense schedules before adding another fusion

The fresh graph experiment puts the sixteen C512 blocks at **2.168032 ms** at
the 4K input anchor. Every C512 block receives B1/H68/W120/C512, or M8160 rows.
Their individual graph medians lie between 134.464 and 136.832 microseconds.
The four phases and block30's required raw output do not create a large timing
outlier. This makes the common dense body the useful next place to investigate.

These are the candidate graph's own block intervals. Native stage boundaries
fuse different work, so a native family total is not a directly matched speed
comparison. The graph-family total is the median of each replay's summed
intervals, not the sum of separately computed medians.

## Evidence and actual selected kernels

Graph evidence:
`profile/trunk-stage-events-fp8-packed-direct-20261003T080534_296106Z/analysis/stage_events.json`.
Eager CUPTI trace:
`profile/network-fp8-packed-20261003T080742_250708Z/reports/trace.json`.
The CPU-only role extraction is saved in
`outputs/c512_public_trace_attribution_v29.json.draft` with the trace hash.
It matches each kernel to its public operator by **External id** and verifies
the six-operation sequence separately for blocks23–30 and40–47. A C512→C256
down-projection is explicitly excluded by its weight shape.

The trace and graph measurements use v29 binary
`54efa3f7bfe3410d3c2320b6494f50262cbb0dd60ae0a5579dabeaf0a2ea8e49`.
The GEMM policy is `ec70c084051c4c3e`, residual policy `61acd20342d20301`,
branch policy `23e446b1701f31ab`, and cached-window policy `86bfe65380016080`.

| Public role | Compute-kernel sum, 16 blocks | Median kernel | Selected schedule/resources |
| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |
| W1 512→512 | 374.597 us | 23.4085 us | BM32/BN64/BK64, scalar shared loads, 38 registers, 15,360 shared bytes |
| Eight branch FFNs | 262.533 us | 16.4320 us | One warp, 78 registers, no shared memory |
| W4 + scaled FFN seed | 298.692 us | 18.6725 us | BM128/BN128/BK64, 102 registers, 40,960 shared bytes |
| QKV 512→1536 | 500.742 us | 31.3445 us | BM128/BN128/BK64, ldmatrix, 79 registers, 40,960 shared bytes |
| Window attention | 299.333 us | 18.6400 us | Four warps, 40 registers, 8,320 shared bytes |
| Projection + scaled attention seed | 306.466 us | 19.0720 us | Same residual tile; 109 registers for block30 raw output, otherwise102 |

These are eager profiler attribution numbers, not CUDA Graph benchmark times.
They must not be added to inclusive CPU operator device-time totals. Projection
also owns sixteen Half→E4 pack launches totaling **38.435 us**. The attention
kernel already publishes E4 values but stores them in Half; the residual API
packs those values back into bytes before its GEMM. W1 has a separate Python
publication pack after its Half-output GEMM. Eliminating either pack could be
useful, but the measured copies are much smaller than the four dense GEMMs.

## The policy has not measured these W1/QKV rows

The exact ungrouped FP8/EP0 W1 and QKV families in `gemm_policy.h` have M320
and M2160 anchors only. M8160 therefore inherits the M2160 choice. W1's policy
variant3 selects the split-capable BM32/BN64 implementation; partition equals K,
so it has only one partition and executes the same unsplit tile as variant2.
This is consistent with the actual mangled CUPTI symbol. QKV selects variant10.
Endpoint clamping is correct behavior, but is not evidence of optimal speed at
the larger row count.

Root's subsequent **exploratory** v30 screen in
`outputs/gemm_anchor_screen_v30.json` supports testing this gap: at M8160,
W1 variant10 measured12.629 us versus variant3's23.968 us, while QKV's existing
variant10 remained fastest at33.24 us. This screen used synthetic inputs,
no residual seed and the older nonpaired graph helper; it establishes neither
a promoted policy nor a whole-block improvement. It also uses a different
binary, `de9ea93be1d9801e8934cf57a0ff498d300230b3aa0dbabe80e806ed97f24c33`.

## Bounded next experiment

First profile block23 at H68/W120 using its **actual public forward**, with all
prepared real weights. Start with W1, then QKV and W4. Projection is a distinct
role even when it shares W4's kernel symbol; block30 adds the raw-output case.
`tuning/profile_c512_public.py.draft` provides this experiment:

```powershell
.venv\Scripts\python.exe tuning/profile_c512_public.py --block 23 --height 68 --width 120 --role w1
.venv\Scripts\python.exe tuning/profile_c512_public.py --block 23 --height 68 --width 120 --role qkv
.venv\Scripts\python.exe tuning/profile_c512_public.py --block 23 --height 68 --width 120 --role w4
.venv\Scripts\python.exe tuning/profile_c512_public.py --block 30 --height 68 --width 120 --role projection
```

These commands require root to activate the draft first. No GPU execution has
been performed by this subtask. Each run archives active source/binary identity
and compiled policies, performs eager and three changed-input/poisoned graph
checks, and captures a standalone CUPTI trace. Separate full/source NCU workers
then call the same public forward. An exact mangled symbol filter and the number
of earlier identical-symbol launches select one role inside the block. Full
collection requests available PM sampling sections; source collection requests
SourceCounters. The existing `ncu_report` parser saves typed counters, per-PC
hotspots and source mappings; cuobjdump saves the exact SM-specific symbol.

The input is deterministic synthetic **published state**, while all weights
are the real extracted block weights and all later operands arise from the
real public block. The correctness oracle uses separate ordered dense/group
GEMMs with the original Half seed and publication points. Attention is shared
with the candidate; this is explicitly a local composition proof, not an
independent attention or original-DLL proof. Reference work and graph checks
remain outside the profiler markers. Cold-cache NCU replay is not resident
graph timing.

Before implementing a kernel, use those profiles to determine whether W1's
small tile suffers repeated shared/input traffic, short tensor issue runs,
address/loop overhead, or a different bottleneck. Compare its existing large
tile alternative with real W1 inputs using paired alternating CUDA Graph
timings, independent ordered outputs, all retained outputs, mutated-input
replays and weight immutability. Cover actual M1056/M2160/M8160 anchors and
several real blocks before generating a measured policy. A new public policy
still needs native trunk reproof under a new binary/journal identity.

Only after this bounded schedule experiment should a new fusion be selected.
A plain packed W1 epilogue could remove its Half temporary/publication kernel;
the generic tiled kernel already has a plain packed epilogue, but the current
public dense API does not expose it. A packed attention output could similarly
remove projection's input repack. Either requires a separate measured experiment
with the same E4 rounding and raw-output contracts. Fusing QKV with attention
is a substantially larger change in data layout, accumulator lifetime and
shared-memory usage; the current evidence does not justify starting there.

No new speedup, native performance parity, or85% roofline gate is claimed here.
